==Old Irish==

===Etymology===
{{root|sga|ine-pro|*ped-}}
From {{inh|sga|cel-pro|*ɸīssu}}, from {{inh|sga|ine-pro||*pedsú}}, locative plural of {{m|ine-pro|*pṓds|t=foot}}.<ref>{{R:cel:EDPC|131}}</ref>

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|sga|/iːs/}}

===Preposition===
{{head|sga|preposition|takes the dative}}

# [[under]], [[below]], [[beneath]]
#* Patrick's Hymn, published in ''Thesaurus Palaeohibernicus'' (reprinted 1975, Dublin Institute for Advanced Studies), edited and with translations by Whitley Stokes and John Strachan, vol.&nbsp;II, p.&nbsp;350, line&nbsp;17
#*: {{quote|sga|Drochet bethad bīd '''íssum''' &emsp; bennacht Dé athar úasum.|Let there be a bridge of life '''beneath me''', [and] the blessing of God the Father above me.}}
#* Patrick's Hymn, published in ''Thesaurus Palaeohibernicus'' (reprinted 1975, Dublin Institute for Advanced Studies), edited and with translations by Whitley Stokes and John Strachan, vol.&nbsp;II, p.&nbsp;357, line&nbsp;15
#*: {{quote|sga|Críst indium, Críst '''íssum''', Críst úasum|Christ in me, Christ '''below me''', Christ above me}}
#* {{RQ:sga:Fel|Epilogue, line 377}}
#*: {{quote|sga|Fom·glúaissi mór ndubai sund '''ís''' riched rindmas...|Much sorrow disquiets me here, '''below''' star-beautiful heaven...}}
#* {{RQ:sga:Glosses|Sg|46|a|8}}
#*: {{quote|sga|'''hís''' bronnait|'''under''' a small belly (glossing {{noncog|la|-}} {{m|la|infra}} {{m|la|ventriculum}})}}
